#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147168 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e0f80f6-920a-5eb0-919c-62c8ba67a2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Multiplication and Division as Fraction Multiplication"}] :question/id #uuid "ba1bbec5-0ff2-52ec-ac2e-876a8ea4c70e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13755" :question/problem "What fraction is missing from the statement below?\n\n$0$" :question/worked-solution "The given expression is\n$(15\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 13}}) \\times (4\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 5}})$.\n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can write an equivalent expression by swapping the ${\\color{red}{\\div\\, 5}}$ and the ${\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 13}}$:\n$(15\\, {\\color{red}{\\div\\, 5}}) \\times (4\\, {\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 13}})$\n\nWe can now interpret the divisions as fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15} 5 \\times \\dfrac 4 {13}\n$$\n\nSo the missing fraction is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15} 5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147169 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2d79438-2593-55c1-a937-c40f8fc6db08" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Expression as an Equivalent Product of Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "ebdd2229-6712-561c-821b-7286460a6824" :question/math-academy-id "e-13758" :question/problem "Find an expression of the form $0$ equivalent to $\\dfrac{4}{3}\\times\\dfrac{9}{2}$." :question/worked-solution "Interpreting the fraction as division, we can write our expression as\n$(4\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 3}}) \\times (9\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 2}})$.\n\nWe use parentheses to make it easier to read.\n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can write an equivalent expression by swapping the ${\\color{red}{\\div\\, 3}}$ and the ${\\color{blue}{\\div\\,2}}$:\n$(4\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\,2}}) \\times (9\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 3}})$\n\nFinally, we can write the $4\\div 2$ and $9\\div 3$ as fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{2} \\times \\dfrac{9}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147170 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8b307c7-79bc-58de-ab88-b0783857371b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the Answer Is a Whole Number"}] :question/id #uuid "830169cc-f331-5a91-81d6-374aad29e951" :question/math-academy-id "e-13761" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\dfrac{12}{7}\\times \\dfrac{21}{4}$." :question/worked-solution "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n$(12\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}})\\times(21\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 4}})$.\n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the ${\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}}$ and ${\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 4}}$:\n$(12\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 4}})\\times(21\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}})$\n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{4}\\times\\dfrac{21}{7}\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{4}_(⏟)_(3) \\cdot \\frac{21}{7}_(⏟)_(3) = 3 \\cdot 3 = 9\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147186 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147407 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee7ebc5-72be-53b1-86d8-27bd939a7244" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the Answer Is a Fraction"}] :question/id #uuid "36b559ef-d696-580d-813f-c80fc9bc36b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-14704" :question/problem "What is $\\dfrac{11}{30}\\times \\dfrac{12}{7}$, expressed as a fraction in lowest terms?" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{\\color{red}30}\\times \\dfrac{\\color{blue}12}{7}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($\\color{red}30$) and the second fraction's numerator ($\\color{blue}12$) have a common\nfactor of $6$.\n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by first swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{7} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{red}12}{\\color{blue}30}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the second fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $6$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{11}{7} \\cdot \\frac{12 \\div 6}{30 \\div 6} &= \\frac{11}{7} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{7}\\times \\dfrac{2}{5} = \\dfrac{11 \\times 2}{7 \\times 5} = \\dfrac{22}{35}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147902 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2d79438-2593-55c1-a937-c40f8fc6db08" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Expression as an Equivalent Product of Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ee886e5-ebd6-509e-817a-293fb9f025a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5} \\cdot \\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186145167} {:answer/id #uuid "ac39b70e-0cd4-5720-94f3-d100e8777bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{1} \\cdot \\frac{12}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186145168} {:answer/id #uuid "f93cf6dd-04f5-56f1-b4df-b02f61ba9728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{5} \\cdot \\frac{12}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186145169} {:answer/id #uuid "49dd1428-3fb0-5b58-9709-e6973969b334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{5} \\cdot \\frac{6}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186145170} {:answer/id #uuid "798db5a8-bcc1-5b66-b811-84744fb1c2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6} \\cdot \\frac{6}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186145171}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f7c6136b-591a-5b86-a6b3-a4de2c8309d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186